  'intezo.guide.summary': '📖 How to use the Explorer — creating, renaming, deleting and linking folders',
  'intezo.guide.body': '<p><strong>The shortest route: RIGHT-CLICK.</strong> Right-click any row in the list — Marveen selects it and offers what you can do with it: open, new folder here, rename, move, link, paper copy, delete. Right-clicking empty space gives you “New folder here".</p><h4>Moving around the tree</h4><ul><li>Click a <strong>folder name</strong> to enter it.</li><li>To go back: the <strong>↑ button</strong> above the list, or click any name in the breadcrumb at the top.</li><li>The note in brackets after a name says <em>what usually goes into that folder</em>.</li></ul><h4>New folder</h4><ul><li>Enter the folder you want it in → <strong>“New folder"</strong> button at the top, or right-click empty space.</li><li>Or right-click the folder you want it inside → <strong>“New folder here"</strong>.</li></ul><h4>Rename, move, delete</h4><ul><li>Right-click the row → <strong>Rename</strong> / <strong>Move</strong> / <strong>Delete</strong>.</li><li>Deleting is <strong>not permanent</strong>: the item goes under <code>System / Trash</code> into a dated folder, and can be brought back from there at any time.</li><li><strong>Permanent delete:</strong> enter the <code>System / Trash</code> folder — there the right-click menu offers <strong>“Delete permanently"</strong>, or use <strong>“Empty the Trash"</strong> on the Trash folder itself. This is the only irreversible operation in the tree, which is why it asks separately.</li><li>The Trash <strong>also empties itself</strong>: anything older than 60 days disappears. The limit is set under <em>Settings → LIFE_TRASH_DAYS</em>, <code>0</code> = never.</li><li>The <strong>main branches</strong> of the tree (Inbox, System, people, Companies) cannot be deleted this way — the configuration creates them, and the next “Create folder structure" would put them back anyway. If you no longer need a person or a project, remove it under <strong>“Who should appear in the tree?"</strong>.</li></ul><h4>Adding a person, company or project</h4><ol><li>At the top: <strong>“Who should appear in the tree?"</strong>.</li><li><strong>“+ Person"</strong> / <strong>“+ Company"</strong>, or inside a person’s box, <strong>“+ Project"</strong> under <strong>“Own projects"</strong>. For a project, leave the <strong>“development (GIT_REPOS)"</strong> box ticked if code belongs to it.</li><li><strong>“Save and preview"</strong> — this is still only a plan; it lists what will be created. A typo can be fixed here without leaving a trace.</li><li>The page jumps to the <strong>“Create folder structure"</strong> button at the top. Press it — that is when the folders are created on disk.</li></ol><h4>Linking a git repository</h4><ol><li>Go here: <code>&lt;person&gt; → Projects → &lt;project&gt; → Development → GIT_REPOS</code> (for a company: <code>Companies → &lt;company&gt; → Development → GIT_REPOS</code>).</li><li>Right-click empty space → <strong>“New folder here"</strong>, named exactly like the repository. This is the hook you hang it on.</li><li>Right-click the new folder → <strong>“Link folder (what should it show)"</strong>.</li><li>Pick the repository from the dropdown, then <strong>“Link here"</strong>.</li></ol><p class="dim">A link is <strong>not a copy</strong>: the repository exists once, under <code>System / Storages / Git</code>, and GIT_REPOS only points at it. Removing the link keeps the files. Only <em>real</em> repositories are listed, so you cannot accidentally link a half-copied folder.</p><h4>What not to touch by hand</h4><ul><li>Folders with a <strong>red name</strong> (📛) lead inside a git repository. There git is the owner: moving files by hand loses their history.</li><li><strong>Never</strong> put a password, API key or token into the tree — those belong in the Vault.</li></ul><h4>Can I delete from Windows Explorer?</h4><p>You can — the depot is an ordinary folder on disk — but there is <strong>no Trash inside Marveen</strong> there, no warning about repositories, and links and the paper register would keep pointing at the deleted path. Deleting from here keeps all of that consistent.</p>',
