  'agents.ctx.role_implementer_tip': 'Carries out the plan received. Speaks up if the plan is wrong, but does not re-plan.',
  'agents.ctx.role_checker': 'checker',
  'agents.ctx.role_checker_tip': 'Reviews finished work. Does not fix it -- finds what is wrong with it.',
  'agents.ctx.clean_start': 'Fresh window for the delegate',
  'agents.ctx.clean_start_tip': 'When this agent issues the FIRST command of a NEW task, it clears the delegate\'s conversation first -- and sends the work package immediately after. So the delegate does not carry the previous task with it. It never clears itself, nor an agent that is working or has unread messages.',
  'agents.ctx.clean_start_confirm': 'This will clear OTHER agents\' conversations when a new task is handed out (immediately before the work package). Turn it on?',
  'agents.ctx.handback': 'Hand the command back if it would run longer than:',
  'agents.ctx.handback_unit': 'seconds',
  'agents.ctx.handback_tip': 'The expensive agent should only run short commands on the machine. If a command would take longer than this (full build, full test run, large log), it hands the job back to the context generator, which returns the RESULT. Long output is what fills a context window, and a cheap model produces the same output. 0 = off.',
  'agents.ctx.handback_hint': 'Recommended: 30. If a command would run longer, the agent hands it back to the context broker.',
  'agents.ctx.help_summary': 'What are these four? (click)',
  'agents.ctx.help_manual_title': '1. Manual buttons (Compact / Clear)',
  'agents.ctx.help_manual': 'Runs when you click, and only then. Compact writes a summary of the conversation: the substance stays, some detail is merged away. Clear throws everything out and the agent starts fresh -- irreversible. This is the finest instrument, because you pick the moment: ideally at the end of a task, once the history is no longer needed. Safe to combine with any of the others.',
  'agents.ctx.help_gate_title': '2. Automatic compaction (the switch above)',
  'agents.ctx.help_gate': 'Every minute the system looks at each agent, and compacts any whose context has grown past the limit you set. It can only run while the agent is NOT working -- on a continuously busy agent the number can climb past the limit until it goes idle. If a compaction demonstrably failed to shrink the context, it will not retry for an hour: it does not escalate to a wipe. Important: the limit must stay below the CLI safety net, or it never runs at all, because the safety net gets there first.',
  'agents.ctx.help_cli_title': '3. CLI safety net (shared, not set here)',
  'agents.ctx.help_cli': 'Claude Code\'s own automatic compaction. It cuts in at any time, mid-work, without asking. Measured, it fires at about 69% of the configured number, not 100%. This is what broke things: set to 100 thousand, it compacted at about 69 thousand -- BELOW every gate threshold -- so no gate could ever run, while agents burned two million tokens a day re-compacting. Hence the high setting now: a safety net, not the default behaviour.',
  'agents.ctx.help_broker_title': '4. Agent-built context (the other switch)',
  'agents.ctx.help_broker': 'The only one that does not clean up afterwards but keeps the window from filling in the first place -- which makes it the cheapest of the four. The designated agent gathers what the others need for the job (which file, which function, the error message verbatim) and passes on only that small package. The others stop searching and start asking. This is not compaction: it does not shorten its own conversation, it builds a small, precise starting point for SOMEONE ELSE. Only ONE agent can be ticked at a time.',
  'agents.ctx.help_checkpoint_title': 'What happens before EVERY compaction (cannot be turned off)',
  'agents.ctx.help_checkpoint': 'However the compaction starts, a save runs right before it: it collects what you asked for (including anything that came in over Telegram), which files were touched and what the next step is, then stores it -- after the compaction that is the first thing the agent gets back, so you never have to repeat yourself. The same run writes one daily-log line and one memory entry. At most once a day per agent, a cheap model (NOT your Claude quota) distills 1-3 durable lessons from it, and if the session taught a genuinely repeatable procedure it writes or extends a skill: it never overwrites an existing one, only appends a dated section, and suspicious content is discarded. If the dashboard happens to be down, the compaction still runs; only this save is skipped.',
  'agents.ctx.help_order': 'All four can run together, on one condition: they must cut in in this order. Manual (you decide) < automatic compaction < CLI safety net. If the automatic limit is set above the safety net, saving is refused, because that setting could never fire. That is the only conflict; the fourth (agent-built context) sits well with all of them, and in fact keeps the context growing slowest.',
